## sn22.6
sn22.6:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.6:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, meditate in retreat. | T: “Bhikkhus, make an exertion in seclusion.
sn22.6:1.3 | S: A mendicant in retreat truly understands. | T: A bhikkhu who is secluded understands things as they really are.
sn22.6:1.4 | S: What do they truly understand? | T: “And what does he understand as it really is?
sn22.6:1.5 | S: The origin and disappearance of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. …” | T: The origin and passing away of form; the origin and passing away of feeling; the origin and passing away of perception; the origin and passing away of volitional formations; the origin and passing away of consciousness. “And what, bhikkhus, is the origin of form?…”
sn22.6:1.6 | S: (Tell in full as in the previous discourse.) | T: The rest of this sutta is identical with the preceding one.
sn22.6:1.7 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.71
sn22.71:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.71:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Rādha went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then the Venerable Radha approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn22.71:1.3 | S: “Sir, how does one know and see so that there’s no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs?” | T: “Venerable sir, how should one know, how should one see so that, in regard to this body with consciousness and in regard to all external signs, I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit no longer occur within?”
sn22.71:1.4 | S: “Rādha, one truly sees any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any kind of form whatsoever, Radha, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, gross or subtle, inferior or superior, far or near—one sees all form as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn22.71:1.5 | S: One truly sees any kind of feeling … | T: “Any kind of feeling whatsoever …
sn22.71:1.6 | S: perception … | T: Any kind of perception whatsoever …
sn22.71:1.7 | S: choices … | T: Any kind of volitional formations whatsoever …
sn22.71:1.8 | S: consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: Any kind of consciousness whatsoever, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, gross or subtle, inferior or superior, far or near—one sees all consciousness as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn22.71:1.9 | S: That’s how to know and see so that there’s no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs.” … | T: “When one knows and sees thus, Radha, then in regard to this body with consciousness and in regard to all external signs, I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit no longer occur within.”
sn22.71:1.10 | S: And Venerable Rādha became one of the perfected. | T: Then the Venerable Radha … became one of the arahants.
sn22.71:1.11 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.72
sn22.72:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.72:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Surādha said to the Buddha: | T: Then the Venerable Suradha approached the Blessed One … and said to him:
sn22.72:1.3 | S: “Sir, how does one know and see so that the heart is rid of I-making, mine-making, and conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs; and going beyond discrimination, it’s peaceful and well freed?” | T: “Venerable sir, how should one know, how should one see so that, in regard to this body with consciousness and in regard to all external signs, the mind is rid of I-making, mine-making, and conceit, has transcended discrimination, and is peaceful and well liberated?”
sn22.72:1.4 | S: “Surādha, one is freed by not grasping having truly seen any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any kind of form whatsoever, Suradha, whether past, future, or present … far or near—having seen all form as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self,’ one is liberated by nonclinging.
sn22.72:1.5 | S: One is freed by not grasping having truly seen any kind of feeling … | T: “Any kind of feeling whatsoever …
sn22.72:1.6 | S: perception … | T: Any kind of perception whatsoever …
sn22.72:1.7 | S: choices … | T: Any kind of volitional formations whatsoever …
sn22.72:1.8 | S: consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: Any kind of consciousness whatsoever, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, gross or subtle, inferior or superior, far or near—having seen all consciousness as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self,’
sn22.72:1.9 | P: sabbā saññā … | T: (none)
sn22.72:1.10 | P: sabbe saṅkhārā … | T: (none)
sn22.72:1.11 | P: sabbaṁ viññāṇaṁ: ‘netaṁ mama, nesohamasmi, na meso attā’ti evametaṁ yathābhūtaṁ sammappaññāya disvā anupādāvimutto hoti. | T: one is liberated by nonclinging.
sn22.72:1.12 | S: That’s how to know and see so that the heart is rid of I-making, mine-making, and conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs; and going beyond discrimination, it’s peaceful and well freed.” … | T: “When one knows and sees thus, Suradha, then in regard to this body with consciousness and in regard to all external signs, the mind is rid of I-making, mine-making, and conceit, has transcended discrimination, and is peaceful and well liberated.”

