## sn22.7
sn22.7:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.7:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you how grasping leads to anxiety, and how not grasping leads to freedom from anxiety. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you agitation through clinging and nonagitation through nonclinging.
sn22.7:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen to that and attend closely, I will speak.”
sn22.7:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied.
sn22.7:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn22.7:2.1 | S: “And how does grasping lead to anxiety? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is there agitation through clinging?
sn22.7:2.2 | S: It’s when an unlearned ordinary person has not seen the noble ones, and is neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve not seen true persons, and are neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the true persons. | T: Here, bhikkhus, the uninstructed worldling, who is not a seer of the noble ones and is unskilled and undisciplined in their Dhamma, who is not a seer of superior persons and is unskilled and undisciplined in their Dhamma,
sn22.7:2.3 | S: They regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: regards form as self, or self as possessing form, or form as in self, or self as in form.
sn22.7:2.4 | S: But that form of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That form of his changes and alters.
sn22.7:2.5 | S: and consciousness latches on to the perishing of form. | T: With the change and alteration of form, his consciousness becomes preoccupied with the change of form.
sn22.7:2.6 | S: Anxieties occupy their mind, born of latching on to the perishing of form, and originating in accordance with natural principles. | T: Agitation and a constellation of mental states born of preoccupation with the change of form remain obsessing his mind.
sn22.7:2.7 | S: So they become frightened, worried, concerned, and anxious because of grasping. | T: Because his mind is obsessed, he is frightened, distressed, and anxious, and through clinging he becomes agitated.
sn22.7:3.1 | S: They regard feeling as self … | T: “He regards feeling as self …
sn22.7:3.2 | P: Tassa sā vedanā vipariṇamati aññathā hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:3.3 | P: Tassa vedanāvipariṇāmaññathābhāvā vedanāvipariṇāmānuparivatti viññāṇaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:3.4 | P: Tassa vedanāvipariṇāmānuparivattijā paritassanā dhammasamuppādā cittaṁ pariyādāya tiṭṭhanti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:3.5 | P: Cetaso pariyādānā uttāsavā ca hoti vighātavā ca apekkhavā ca upādāya ca paritassati. | T: (none)
sn22.7:4.1 | S: They regard perception as self … | T: perception as self …
sn22.7:4.2 | S: They regard choices as self … | T: volitional formations as self …
sn22.7:4.3 | P: Tassa te saṅkhārā vipariṇamanti aññathā honti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:4.4 | P: Tassa saṅkhāravipariṇāmaññathābhāvā saṅkhāravipariṇāmānuparivatti viññāṇaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:4.5 | P: Tassa saṅkhāravipariṇāmānuparivattijā paritassanā dhammasamuppādā cittaṁ pariyādāya tiṭṭhanti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:4.6 | P: Cetaso pariyādānā uttāsavā ca hoti vighātavā ca apekkhavā ca upādāya ca paritassati. | T: (none)
sn22.7:5.1 | S: They regard consciousness as self, self as having consciousness, consciousness in self, or self in consciousness. | T: consciousness as self, or self as possessing consciousness, or consciousness as in self, or self as in consciousness.
sn22.7:5.2 | S: But that consciousness of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That consciousness of his changes and alters.
sn22.7:5.3 | S: and consciousness latches on to the perishing of consciousness. | T: With the change and alteration of consciousness, his consciousness becomes preoccupied with the change of consciousness.
sn22.7:5.4 | S: Anxieties occupy their mind, born of latching on to the perishing of consciousness, and originating in accordance with natural principles. | T: Agitation and a constellation of mental states born of preoccupation with the change of consciousness remain obsessing his mind.
sn22.7:5.5 | S: So they become frightened, worried, concerned, and anxious because of grasping. | T: Because his mind is obsessed, he is frightened, distressed, and anxious, and through clinging he becomes agitated.
sn22.7:5.6 | S: That’s how grasping leads to anxiety. | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that there is agitation through clinging.
sn22.7:6.1 | S: And how does not grasping lead to freedom from anxiety? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is there nonagitation through nonclinging?
sn22.7:6.2 | S: It’s when a learned noble disciple has seen the noble ones, and is skilled and trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve seen true persons, and are skilled and trained in the teaching of the true persons. | T: Here, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple, who is a seer of the noble ones and is skilled and disciplined in their Dhamma, who is a seer of superior persons and is skilled and disciplined in their Dhamma,
sn22.7:6.3 | S: They don’t regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: does not regard form as self, or self as possessing form, or form as in self, or self as in form.
sn22.7:6.4 | S: When that form of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That form of his changes and alters.
sn22.7:6.5 | S: consciousness doesn’t latch on to the perishing of form. | T: Despite the change and alteration of form, his consciousness does not become preoccupied with the change of form.
sn22.7:6.6 | S: Anxieties—born of latching on to the perishing of form and originating in accordance with natural principles—don’t occupy their mind. | T: No agitation and constellation of mental states born of preoccupation with the change of form remain obsessing his mind.
sn22.7:6.7 | S: So they don’t become frightened, worried, concerned, or anxious because of grasping. | T: Because his mind is not obsessed, he is not frightened, distressed, or anxious, and through nonclinging he does not become agitated.
sn22.7:7.1 | S: They don’t regard feeling as self … | T: “He does not regard feeling as self …
sn22.7:7.2 | P: Tassa sā vedanā vipariṇamati aññathā hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:7.3 | P: Tassa vedanāvipariṇāmaññathābhāvā na vedanāvipariṇāmānuparivatti viññāṇaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:7.4 | P: Tassa na vedanāvipariṇāmānuparivattijā paritassanā dhammasamuppādā cittaṁ pariyādāya tiṭṭhanti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:7.5 | P: Cetaso apariyādānā na cevuttāsavā hoti na ca vighātavā na ca apekkhavā, anupādāya ca na paritassati. | T: (none)
sn22.7:8.1 | S: They don’t regard perception as self … | T: perception as self …
sn22.7:8.2 | S: They don’t regard choices as self … | T: volitional formations as self …
sn22.7:8.3 | P: na saṅkhāravantaṁ vā attānaṁ; | T: …
sn22.7:8.4 | P: Tassa te saṅkhārā vipariṇamanti aññathā honti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:8.5 | P: Tassa saṅkhāravipariṇāmaññathābhāvā na saṅkhāravipariṇāmānuparivatti viññāṇaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:8.6 | P: Tassa na saṅkhāravipariṇāmānuparivattijā paritassanā dhammasamuppādā cittaṁ pariyādāya tiṭṭhanti. | T: (none)
sn22.7:8.7 | P: Cetaso apariyādānā na cevuttāsavā hoti na ca vighātavā na ca apekkhavā, anupādāya ca na paritassati. | T: (none)
sn22.7:9.1 | S: They don’t regard consciousness as self … | T: consciousness as self, or self as possessing consciousness, or consciousness as in self, or self as in consciousness.
sn22.7:9.2 | S: When that consciousness of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That consciousness of his changes and alters.
sn22.7:9.3 | S: consciousness doesn’t latch on to the perishing of consciousness. | T: Despite the change and alteration of consciousness, his consciousness does not become preoccupied with the change of consciousness.
sn22.7:9.4 | S: Anxieties—born of latching on to the perishing of consciousness and originating in accordance with natural principles—don’t occupy their mind. | T: No agitation and constellation of mental states born of preoccupation with the change of consciousness remain obsessing his mind.
sn22.7:9.5 | S: So they don’t become frightened, worried, concerned, or anxious because of grasping. | T: Because his mind is not obsessed, he is not frightened, distressed, or anxious, and through nonclinging he does not become agitated.
sn22.7:9.6 | S: That’s how not grasping leads to freedom from anxiety.” | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that there is nonagitation through nonclinging.”
sn22.7:9.7 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.8
sn22.8:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.8:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you how grasping leads to anxiety, and how not grasping leads to freedom from anxiety. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you agitation through clinging and nonagitation through nonclinging.
sn22.8:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak. | T: Listen to that and attend closely….
sn22.8:1.4 | S: And how does grasping lead to anxiety? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is there agitation through clinging?
sn22.8:1.5 | S: It’s when an unlearned ordinary person regards form like this: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self.’ | T: Here, bhikkhus, the uninstructed worldling regards form thus: ‘This is mine, this I am, this is my self.’
sn22.8:1.6 | S: But that form of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That form of his changes and alters. With the change and alteration of form,
sn22.8:1.7 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair.
sn22.8:1.8 | S: They regard feeling … | T: “He regards feeling thus …
sn22.8:1.9 | S: perception … | T: perception thus …
sn22.8:1.10 | S: choices … | T: volitional formations thus …
sn22.8:1.11 | S: consciousness like this: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self.’ | T: consciousness thus: ‘This is mine, this I am, this is my self.’
sn22.8:1.12 | S: But that consciousness of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That consciousness of his changes and alters. With the change and alteration of consciousness,
sn22.8:1.13 | S: which gives rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: there arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair.
sn22.8:1.14 | S: That’s how grasping leads to anxiety. | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that there is agitation through clinging.
sn22.8:2.1 | S: And how does not grasping lead to freedom from anxiety? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, is there nonagitation through nonclinging?
sn22.8:2.2 | S: It’s when a learned noble disciple regards form like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: Here, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple does not regard form thus: ‘This is mine, this I am, this is my self.’
sn22.8:2.3 | S: When that form of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That form of his changes and alters. With the change and alteration of form,
sn22.8:2.4 | S: it doesn’t give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: there do not arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair.
sn22.8:2.5 | S: They regard feeling … | T: “He does not regard feeling thus …
sn22.8:2.6 | S: perception … | T: perception thus …
sn22.8:2.7 | S: choices … | T: volitional formations thus …
sn22.8:2.8 | S: consciousness like this: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: consciousness thus: ‘This is mine, this I am, this is my self.’
sn22.8:2.9 | S: When that consciousness of theirs decays and perishes, | T: That consciousness of his changes and alters. With the change and alteration of consciousness,
sn22.8:2.10 | S: it doesn’t give rise to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: there do not arise in him sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair.
sn22.8:2.11 | S: That’s how not grasping leads to freedom from anxiety.” | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that there is nonagitation through nonclinging.”
sn22.8:2.12 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.91
sn22.91:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.91:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Rāhula went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then the Venerable Rahula approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn22.91:1.3 | S: “Sir, how does one know and see so that there’s no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs?” | T: “Venerable sir, how should one know, how should one see so that, in regard to this body with consciousness and in regard to all external signs, I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit no longer occur within?”
sn22.91:2.1 | S: “Rāhula, one truly sees any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Any kind of form whatsoever, Rahula, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, gross or subtle, inferior or superior, far or near—one sees all form as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn22.91:2.2 | S: One truly sees any kind of feeling … | T: “Any kind of feeling whatsoever …
sn22.91:2.3 | S: perception … | T: Any kind of perception whatsoever …
sn22.91:2.4 | S: choices … | T: Any kind of volitional formations whatsoever …
sn22.91:2.5 | S: consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: Any kind of consciousness whatsoever, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, gross or subtle, inferior or superior, far or near—one sees all consciousness as it really is with correct wisdom thus: ‘This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self.’
sn22.91:2.6 | P: sabbaṁ viññāṇaṁ ‘netaṁ mama, nesohamasmi, na meso attā’ti evametaṁ yathābhūtaṁ sammappaññāya passati. | T: (none)
sn22.91:2.7 | S: That’s how to know and see so that there’s no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs.” | T: “When one knows and sees thus, Rahula, then in regard to this body with consciousness and in regard to all external signs, I-making, mine-making, and the underlying tendency to conceit no longer occur within.”
sn22.91:2.8 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)


